WEBVTT

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.800
[Merc sings an A, 220 Hz]

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:05.050
[Tavi sings an A, 440 Hz]

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:06.809
TAVI: Ha! Same note.

00:00:07.109 --> 00:00:11.612
MERC: Is it?
Mine shook the air 220 times a second.

00:00:11.862 --> 00:00:14.512
Yours shook it 440.

00:00:14.812 --> 00:00:18.388
TAVI: Twice as fast?
Then why do they sound the same?

00:00:18.788 --> 00:00:23.567
MERC: Good question. To answer it,
we need to know what a note really is.

00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:26.967
[chime]

00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:32.070
Every note you have ever heard
starts with something shaking.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:33.783
A guitar string.

00:00:34.083 --> 00:00:35.147
A speaker.

00:00:35.447 --> 00:00:37.759
The vocal folds in your throat.

00:00:38.609 --> 00:00:43.259
Pluck a string, and it swings
back and forth, pushing on the air.

00:00:43.559 --> 00:00:46.018
Here it is, slowed right down.

00:00:46.418 --> 00:00:50.868
Each push travels outward as a wave,
all the way to your ear.

00:00:51.268 --> 00:00:55.598
Count the swings in one second,
and you have the note's frequency.

00:00:56.198 --> 00:01:01.253
At real speed,
this string swings 220 times.

00:01:01.553 --> 00:01:04.797
We measure it in hertz. Swings per second.

00:01:05.597 --> 00:01:07.862
Slow swings make a low note.

00:01:07.945 --> 00:01:09.897
[a tone sweeps up from 110 to 880 Hz]

00:01:10.197 --> 00:01:12.438
Fast swings make a high one.

00:01:13.038 --> 00:01:15.771
TAVI: So a high note
is just a loud note?

00:01:16.071 --> 00:01:16.904
MERC: No.

00:01:16.997 --> 00:01:19.169
Loud is how far it swings.

00:01:19.619 --> 00:01:21.266
High is how fast.

00:01:21.966 --> 00:01:24.963
MercuryPitch draws
your voice the same way.

00:01:25.046 --> 00:01:28.341
As you sing,
a line climbs when you go higher.

00:01:31.141 --> 00:01:33.109
Now, back to our puzzle.

00:01:33.459 --> 00:01:35.552
Here is 220.

00:01:35.852 --> 00:01:37.937
And here is 440.

00:01:38.237 --> 00:01:40.626
Exactly twice as fast.

00:01:41.126 --> 00:01:46.865
Before you listen, predict.
Two different notes, or the same one?

00:01:47.165 --> 00:01:49.765
[pause and predict]

00:01:49.965 --> 00:01:51.365
[220 Hz]

00:01:51.665 --> 00:01:53.065
[440 Hz]

00:01:53.365 --> 00:01:55.365
[both together]

00:01:55.865 --> 00:01:59.955
Higher, yes. But together,
they melt into one note.

00:02:00.305 --> 00:02:01.884
That jump has a name.

00:02:02.234 --> 00:02:03.434
An octave.

00:02:04.234 --> 00:02:05.671
Now look at the waves.

00:02:05.754 --> 00:02:10.730
Every second peak of the fast one
lands right on a peak of the slow one.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:14.726
They line up again, and again, forever.

00:02:15.226 --> 00:02:19.924
TAVI: Like us walking together.
I take two steps for every one of yours.

00:02:20.224 --> 00:02:25.892
MERC: And we land together, every time.
Two for one: the simplest fit there is.

00:02:25.975 --> 00:02:27.393
That's the octave.

00:02:28.143 --> 00:02:35.724
Try 277 instead, and the peaks drift in
and out of step. That's a different note.

00:02:36.224 --> 00:02:39.098
Go down an octave, to 110.

00:02:39.348 --> 00:02:40.265
[A: 110 to 880 Hz]

00:02:40.348 --> 00:02:45.583
Then double, and double, and double.
The same letter, every time.

00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:51.473
The gaps in hertz keep doubling,
but every jump sounds the same size.

00:02:51.557 --> 00:02:54.679
Your ear hears multiplying, not adding.

00:02:57.579 --> 00:03:01.683
So pitch isn't really a straight line.
It's more like a spiral.

00:03:02.083 --> 00:03:06.463
Go up an octave, and you come back round,
right above where you started.

00:03:07.063 --> 00:03:11.550
Musicians name the notes on
each turn with seven letters.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:16.550
[eight notes: A B C D E F G A]

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.480
TAVI: Only seven notes?

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:20.230
MERC: Seven letters.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:21.710
Twelve notes.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.670
The other five are next time's story.

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:30.633
A number tells you which turn you're on.
Each new turn starts at C.

00:03:31.033 --> 00:03:37.442
So the A that orchestras
tune to is A4. 440 Hz.

00:03:37.742 --> 00:03:43.724
Middle C on a piano is C4.
Same letters, different turns.

00:03:44.724 --> 00:03:47.884
Unroll the spiral, and you get the piano.

00:03:47.967 --> 00:03:51.448
The same seven letters,
octave after octave.

00:03:52.448 --> 00:03:57.685
When two people sing the same tune,
they're often an octave apart. Listen.

00:03:57.985 --> 00:04:02.585
[a tune, two voices an octave apart]

00:04:02.935 --> 00:04:05.391
Two voices, one tune.

00:04:05.691 --> 00:04:10.170
So when you match a note,
an octave up or down still counts.

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:15.553
Let's try. I'll sing an A.
Hum it back, wherever it feels easy.

00:04:15.953 --> 00:04:18.153
[Merc sings an A]

00:04:18.353 --> 00:04:21.753
[your turn: hum an A]

00:04:21.953 --> 00:04:23.953
[Tavi hums an A, an octave up]

00:04:24.203 --> 00:04:26.403
TAVI: I hummed it higher.
Does that count?

00:04:26.665 --> 00:04:31.870
MERC: Higher, but the same note.
That counts. Keep it gentle. Never push.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:33.781
Quick check.

00:04:34.081 --> 00:04:36.571
What makes a note higher?

00:04:36.871 --> 00:04:38.671
[think]

00:04:38.971 --> 00:04:41.699
Faster swings. A higher frequency.

00:04:42.299 --> 00:04:44.556
Double the frequency, and you get?

00:04:44.856 --> 00:04:46.656
[think]

00:04:46.956 --> 00:04:49.105
TAVI: The same note, one octave up!

00:04:49.705 --> 00:04:53.292
MERC: And why do A3 and A4 share a letter?

00:04:53.592 --> 00:04:55.392
[think]

00:04:55.692 --> 00:05:00.979
Their waves fit two to one.
Same spot on the spiral, one turn higher.

00:05:01.979 --> 00:05:02.895
Next time.

00:05:03.195 --> 00:05:07.602
Seven letters, but twelve notes.
Where do the other five come from?

00:05:08.002 --> 00:05:09.102
TAVI: See you there.

00:05:09.185 --> 00:05:13.949
[chime]
